statement:
The matrix `grid` is a shop floor. A cell holding `0` is a wall you cannot enter, a cell holding `1` is an empty aisle, and a cell holding any larger number is an item with that price. You stand at `start = [row, col]` and may step up, down, left or right into any non-wall cell; each step counts as 1.

You only care about items whose price lies in `pricing = [low, high]` (both ends included) and that you can actually reach. Rank such items by these keys, using the next key only when all earlier ones tie:

1. shorter walking distance from `start` (the starting cell itself has distance 0) ranks higher;
2. then the lower price;
3. then the smaller row index;
4. then the smaller column index.

Return the positions `[r, c]` of the `k` best-ranked items, best first. If fewer than `k` such items exist, return all of them.

validator:
function validate([grid, pricing, start, k]) {
  const m = grid.length;
  if (m < 1) return 'grid needs at least one row.';
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (n < 1) return 'Each row needs at least one cell.';
  if (m * n > 100000) return 'grid has at most 10^5 cells.';
  for (const row of grid) {
    if (row.length !== n) return 'All rows of grid must have the same length.';
    for (const v of row) if (!Number.isInteger(v) || v < 0 || v > 100000) return 'Each cell is an integer from 0 to 10^5.';
  }
  if (pricing.length !== 2) return 'pricing is [low, high].';
  const [low, high] = pricing;
  if (!(2 <= low && low <= high && high <= 100000)) return 'pricing needs 2 <= low <= high <= 10^5.';
  if (start.length !== 2) return 'start is [row, col].';
  const [r, c] = start;
  if (!Number.isInteger(r) || !Number.isInteger(c) || r < 0 || r >= m || c < 0 || c >= n) return 'start must be inside the grid.';
  if (grid[r][c] <= 0) return 'The start cell cannot be a wall.';
  if (!Number.isInteger(k) || k < 1 || k > m * n) return 'k is between 1 and m * n.';
  return null;
}

